/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407550 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407550 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407550 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407550 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69f0b9bd-9336-5c1c-91e3-bf72b33b1333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407550 #edb/ref 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407552 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407552 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407552 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407552 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407552 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d231abd-db3d-5d11-9253-820e7eb51742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407552 #edb/ref 1074 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407554 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "07386288-c5f3-55df-b73a-99cec25cb7d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407554 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407555 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407555 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d35b336-2654-53f7-8116-4689735d233e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407555 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407556 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407556 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5fc71f89-cc9a-549d-9f1f-b3a2081e4a63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407556 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407557 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407557 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "61132d09-e84e-51b9-b83a-aedca27c1563" #edb/r